# Cloudflare HTTP headers control (`_headers`), read verbatim by Cloudflare
# Pages and by Workers static assets.
#
# TEN RULES, AND IT STAYS TEN however many puzzles the catalog holds.
# Cloudflare caps a `_headers` file at 100 rules — the same 100 on Pages and on
# Workers static assets, on every plan, so neither migrating nor paying raises
# it. This file used to emit one rule per puzzle entry page, which made that cap
# a cap on the number of GAMES: 71 rules at 57 puzzles, 100 at around 88.
# Nothing about a cache policy needs to know how many puzzles there are.
#
# The coupling came from which way round the default ran. Cloudflare MERGES the
# headers of every rule an incoming request matches, and a header named twice is
# comma-joined — a broad `Cache-Control` plus a narrow one yields
# `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate, no-cache`, which is why the broad
# default sat commented out for so long. `! Cache-Control` detaches the
# inherited value, letting a narrow rule replace rather than append. Defaulting
# `/*` to the LONG cache therefore made every short-cached path an override —
# and the short-cached paths are the entry pages, one per game.
#
# So the default is inverted: `/*` carries the SHORT cache the entry pages want,
# and the few paths wanting a long one detach it. `/`, `/pegs` and `/help/*`
# then need no rule at all, because inheriting is already the right answer.
#
# Inverting also steps around cloudflare/workers-sdk#11351 (open as of
# 2026-09-07), where `!` fails to detach on the root path `/` and the response
# carries both values. Under the old direction `/` needed exactly that override;
# under this one `/` is the default and detaches nothing.
#
# The rule count is not left to this comment: the `_headers` transform in
# `vite.config.ts` counts the rendered rules and fails `vite build` — which is
# in the commit gate — before the file can reach the cap.

{{! Everything, unless a rule below detaches and replaces it. The short cache is
    what the HTML entry points want: `/`, the 57 puzzle pages, `/help/*`, the
    manifest and the JSON. }}
/*
{{#each securityHeaders}}
  {{{ @key }}}: {{{ this }}}
{{/each}}
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, must-revalidate

{{! Static assets with hashed filenames: cache "forever". }}
/assets/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31556952, immutable

/preflight/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31556952, immutable

{{! Favicons, robots.txt and friends. Unhashed, so not immutable, but they
    change far more rarely than a page. `:file` matches one path segment, so
    these reach the root only — a hashed png under /assets/ keeps the rule
    above. }}
/:file.png
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate

/:file.ico
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate

/:file.svg
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate

/:file.txt
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate

{{! Service worker: always check for the latest version (the browser may apply
    its own policy on top). }}
/sw.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: no-cache

{{! Discourage indexing of the preview deployments, so only the canonical
    domain is indexed. }}
https://:project.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
https://:version.:project.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

{{! HSTS, scoped to the domain we own.

    Deliberately a rule here rather than the Cloudflare zone toggle: the same
    mechanism the two rules above already use, and it keeps the security
    posture a fact of the repository instead of dashboard state nobody can see
    from a checkout.

    Scoped by absolute URL because `pages.dev` is NOT ours — it is shared with
    every other Cloudflare Pages project, and pinning HTTPS for a host we do
    not control is not ours to do. Absolute rules must begin with `https`, and
    matching ignores the request's port and protocol.

    RAMPING, so read before raising. A browser that has seen this refuses plain
    HTTP for the whole max-age, which is what makes HSTS awkward to undo: the
    lever is in browsers already in the field, not here. So it starts at five
    minutes. Once the custom domain is verified serving on HTTPS, raise it to
    `max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains` in one step and re-verify.

    NO `preload`. That submits the domain to a browser-vendor list which is
    slow and painful to leave, and it is a decision to take deliberately once
    the domain has been stable for a while — never as part of a ramp. }}
https://hintful.click/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=300
